# R21-DEBT: issue 74 — the last two non-atomic sentinels, and a fourth site that never existed

`DEBT(R21)` is gone from `pipeline/data.py`, because the debt is.

## The block named four sites. Two were real.

| the block's claim | what the code says |
| --- | --- |
| `cli.py` `_write_stage_manifest` | real, and migrated here. It is the helper the rollout and branch stage outputs go through. |
| `cli.py` `health.json` | real, and migrated here, first, for the reason below. |
| `probes/state_probe.py:2845` "duplicates this helper" | **there is no manifest write in that file, and there never was.** `git log -S'manifest.json'` on it is empty across its whole history. |

The third was wrong when the comment was written, and issue 74 inherited the error from the
comment. Which is the argument for deleting a debt comment rather than updating it: a comment
naming work is a queue nobody audits, and this one had been sending readers to a file that had
nothing to fix.

## `health.json` differs in kind from the other sentinels

For `manifest.json` the hazard is downstream. `guard_dataset_write` decides whether a later write
is permitted by whether the file is there, so a truncated one waves a write straight over rollout
data that is in fact complete — the reasoning `branch.py` and `rollout.py` already carry.

`health.json` has no downstream. It is the R26 training-health record, the file a reader opens to
decide whether a run is usable, written from the stage's `finally` at the end of a run that took
seventy minutes. Nothing is waved past by a truncated one. **The record itself is the loss**, and
it is lost at the moment the information is least reproducible. That is why it went first.

## What the tests assert

Two interruption tests and one that the comment is gone. All three fail against the un-migrated
code, checked by reverting `cli.py` and `data.py` and running them.

`_interrupt_the_manifest_writer` is **imported from `test_branch.py`, not copied.** It truncates
both writers — `runlog`'s `os.fdopen` and `Path.write_text` — so the assertion rests on the outcome
rather than on which writer is in use, which is exactly what makes it fail if a site is reverted to
a plain stamp. A second copy could drift, and a copy that stopped patching `Path.write_text` would
keep passing while proving nothing. Neither file loads MLX, so the import does not move
`test_cli.py` into `_TESTS_THAT_LOAD_MLX`.

The health test drives `stage_train` rather than calling the write directly, because the write
happens on the way out of a `finally` block and the question is what survives that path.

The third test also asserts `manifest.json` appears nowhere in `state_probe.py`, so if a stamp is
ever added there it needs the same treatment and this test is the place that says so.

## Out of scope, named rather than done

- **A second `DEBT(R21)` survives, in `chat_replay.py:203`**, and it should. It states a reason
  this one did not: the rows and manifest sit behind a weights-gated path no test can drive, so
  the change needs a run to verify. That is a blocked item with its blocker written down, not an
  unaudited queue.
- A wider audit found other non-atomic JSON writes that no rule covers —
  `preflight.py:354` writes the artifact a training run's gate reads, and `provenance.py:104`
  writes the provenance record. Neither is in this block or this issue. `preflight.py`'s is the
  one I would look at next if you want it; it is a gate input, so it is closer to `health.json`
  in kind than to the sentinels.

